A table others refer to is renamed, and schema.sql's child still names the
old one in its REFERENCES. (The step's rename rewrites the database's own
REFERENCES; schema.sql is the person's to fix.) With no child rows, the
migration would go through and every later insert into the child would
fail. roux-db checks every REFERENCES as the schema builds, and says so
there.

== before
CREATE TABLE menu (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
CREATE TABLE course (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, menu_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES menu (id), name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
INSERT INTO menu VALUES (1, 'lunch');

== after
CREATE TABLE meal (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;
CREATE TABLE course (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, menu_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES menu (id), name TEXT NOT NULL) STRICT;

== ledger
ALTER TABLE menu RENAME TO meal;

== error
roux-db: table course's REFERENCES: no such table: main.menu
